"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', a 1 trillion-parameter natively multimodal open-weights model. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a natively multimodal model with 49 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters, delivering exceptional performance across coding, agentic workflows, and multimodal understanding. Trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its proprietary European data center, its open weights are scheduled for official release at the end of this month. It delivers top-tier open-model performance in enterprise domains such as cybersecurity, finance, and legal, allowing organizations to autonomously deploy and operate it under their own governance policies. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI startup Mistral has established a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest economy—and officially launched its push into the manufacturing and industrial AI market. Mistral officially opened a new hub in Munich, Germany, deploying industrial AI and Physics AI research teams alongside field engineers. The company is collaborating closely with BMW in crash simulation and engineering AI, and with Siemens Energy in developing industrial AI applications. Aiming to secure Europe's AI sovereignty, it plans to build 1 gigawatt (GW) of compute capacity within Europe by 2030 and support independent infrastructure deployments based on open-weight architectures. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have partnered to integrate Mistral AI models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window,' delivering privacy-focused, multilingual browsing capabilities. Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta),' is powered by Mistral models, supporting features such as synthesizing complex searches and tab-based information retrieval. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with support expected to expand to the United Kingdom and Germany later this year. It provides a localized AI experience by applying multilingual models fine-tuned to regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts. User privacy is strongly protected as conversations are not stored on Mozilla's servers by default, and Mistral has agreed to a zero data retention policy. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI have partnered to deliver a Sovereign AI solution that enables enterprises to build customized intelligence while maintaining data sovereignty. Mistral's AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, allowing inference to run under complete control across on-premises, public/private cloud, and air-gapped environments. It helps enterprises maintain full ownership of their data and intelligence by training their own models using large-scale proprietary data within controlled environments. It targets demand from enterprises in regulated industries—such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications—seeking to move beyond renting generic AI to building specialized AI under their own control. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Covers a case study where Mistral AI agents were leveraged to successfully modernize a complex, 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy codebase—lacking tests and documentation—from a European energy company into modern C++. Went beyond simple syntax translation to perform architectural refactoring from a procedural language to object-oriented C++ and integrated the PETSc framework. Built a parity test harness for numerical validation, verifying the accuracy of both intermediate and final computational results. Documented the codebase using AI agents prior to migration, ensuring code quality through a systematic workflow combined with human review. OpenAI’s Alexander Embiricos is coming to TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 — days after the launch of Dots Alexander Embiricos from OpenAI will be speaking at the AI Stage of TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 shortly after the launch of Dots. Alexander Embiricos from OpenAI will attend the AI Stage at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. This appearance comes just days after the launch of OpenAI's new project, Dots. Registration for TechCrunch Disrupt event passes and promotional discounts are currently available. Get hands-on: The full lineup of interactive roundtables at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Introduces the full schedule of major interactive roundtables and registration discount benefits at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. The roundtable lineup featuring major companies and investment firms such as Nvidia, Chime, Obvious Ventures, and Anthropic has been unveiled. You can save up to $100 on pass purchases with advance registration. A 50% discount is offered on the purchase of a second pass. Google’s new SynthID website can identify AI-generated media Google has officially launched the SynthID website, allowing anyone to determine whether media such as images, videos, and audio are AI-generated. The SynthID detection tool, which was restricted to select journalists and researchers at last year's Google I/O, is now fully accessible to the general public. It supports various formats of image, video, and audio files, and along with Google's proprietary models (Nano Banana, Veo, Lyria, etc.) and tools, companies such as OpenAI, Nvidia, and Kakao also support SynthID. Google has integrated the verification feature into the Gemini app and Chrome, and it currently processes 1 million verification requests per day. Google invests millions in Mark Zuckerberg’s efforts to create a ‘virtual cell’ Google DeepMind, Meta, and Isomorphic Labs jointly invested $300 million in Biohub with the goal of building virtual cells for biological simulation. Google DeepMind, Meta, and Isomorphic Labs jointly invested $300 million in Biohub, a non-profit biomedical research institute founded by Mark Zuckerberg and Priscilla Chan. The investment is part of a $1.8 billion initiative to build AI datasets that enable digital biological inquiries and predictions to assist in disease prevention and management. The U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) will contribute more than $500 million over the next five years, and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) will also provide datasets based on over $500 million in prior federal investments. AI could upend food delivery Changes in the food delivery industry and the crisis of platform business models examined through the emergence of the AI agent-based startup Bites and its conflict with DoorDash. As AI agents handle orders and tasks on behalf of users, existing delivery apps and websites face the risk of losing direct touchpoints with consumers and control over upselling. Wary of Bites, a 10-person nascent AI delivery startup, DoorDash sent warning letters to its partner restaurants alleging that Bites had listed restaurants in the Bay Area without authorization. Bites adopts a differentiation strategy to reduce the price burden on restaurants and consumers by offering a flat fee of $1 per order, in contrast to DoorDash's hefty commission rates of 15% to 30%. ChatGPT for Teens is an ‘unacceptable risk,’ says Common Sense Media The nonprofit organization Common Sense Media assessed that OpenAI's ChatGPT for Teens poses an unacceptable level of risk due to inadequate safety guardrails. Common Sense Media pointed out that ChatGPT for Teens falls short of promised protective features, such as failing to properly alert parents in crisis situations and still doing homework on behalf of students. Director Tom Siegel stated that a teenager can talk about self-harm for an hour without any notification being sent to their parents, arguing that the service should remain adult-only until improvements are made. OpenAI countered that the test results were inaccurate because the evaluation was conducted before parental control features were fully activated; however, Common Sense Media reaffirmed the validity of their test results, noting that alerts were not triggered even on accounts linked over an extended period. ML Engineer, AI Engineer, or LLM Engineer: Which Role Actually Builds What in 2026? An article that compares and analyzes the actual responsibilities and development deliverables of ML Engineer, AI Engineer, and LLM Engineer roles, which are often used interchangeably in job postings. Machine Learning Engineers are responsible for collecting and cleaning data, directly training models, and deploying and maintaining them in large-scale production environments. AI Engineers focus on connecting and integrating pre-trained model APIs into actual service products, such as customer support tools, internal enterprise search features, and agents. LLM Engineers specialize in large language models, going beyond API integration to deeply perform fine-tuning of pre-trained model weights using techniques like LoRA. I Tested 5 AI Coding Assistants for a Month: Here’s What I Actually Found An article analyzing the differences in philosophy and real-world performance among five major AI coding assistants through hands-on testing in actual development environments for a month. Each AI coding tool takes a distinct approach: full editor overhaul (Cursor), enhancing existing environments (GitHub Copilot), terminal-based collaboration (Claude Code), autonomous execution (Windsurf), and browser-based one-stop development (Replit Agent). While Cursor excels in multi-file awareness and refactoring, it shows limitations such as modifying code outside the intended scope during ambiguous tasks in large codebases. Moving beyond traditional inline code autocomplete, the autonomy of agent modes—capable of multi-file editing and executing terminal commands—is emerging as a key differentiator in practical workflows. GAMEGO: Training Game-Dev Agents with Synthetic Trajectories Anchored in Real-World Assets This research introduces GAMEGO, a framework for training game development agents using synthetic trajectory data anchored in real-world industrial assets to develop high-quality games from brief input prompts. Systematically transforms simple game seed inputs into detailed product requirement documents (PRDs) aligned with industry standards, and enables design exploration while preserving constraints via dynamic compression techniques Constructs GameGoData, consisting of 55,060 development trajectories spanning 2D, 2.5D, and 3D games, along with the GameGoBench benchmark featuring 124 diverse game queries Demonstrates that the GameGoCoder model trained on this data outperforms existing baseline models and rivals state-of-the-art frontier models in game development performance FluidPD: In-Place Elasticity for SLO-Aware Prefill-Decode Disaggregated LLM Serving This study presents FluidPD, an in-place elasticity system that enhances SLO attainment in LLM serving environments by addressing dynamic imbalances between the prefill and decode stages in real time. Existing prefill-decode disaggregation (P/D disaggregation) architectures have limitations where fixed worker ratios cause latency SLO violations during short-term bursts or sustained demand shifts, even when idle resources exist. FluidPD combines FluidToken, which offloads part of the prefill computation when decode workers have spare capacity, with FluidRole, a mechanism that switches the roles of running workers without reloading models. It proactively detects resource pressure using lightweight pressure metrics and flexibly reallocates resources in an in-place manner without provisioning additional GPUs. Anchor Divergence for Semantic Geometry in Contrastive Learning This paper proposes Anchor Divergences to overcome the limitations of fixed cosine similarity in contrastive representation spaces and to define context-dependent semantic geometry. It highlights that semantic similarity varies depending on context, unlike the fixed geometry based on conventional cosine similarity. By leveraging the interplay among contrastive learning, exponential families, and information geometry, it establishes a correspondence between anchor probability distributions and Bregman geometries. Through retrieval experiments, it demonstrates that modeling anchor distributions enables the efficient definition of context-specific geometries even within fixed representation spaces. RadOnc-Agent: An LLM-Orchestrated Framework for AI Workflows Across the Radiotherapy Care Pathway This study presents RadOnc-Agent, an agentic framework that integrates and orchestrates fragmented AI capabilities across the entire radiotherapy care pathway via a large language model (LLM) controller. Developed the RadOnc-Agent framework, which formalizes the radiotherapy care pathway into four clinical stages and provides 26 functions callable through a conversational interface. Achieved a 98.79% selection accuracy for single-function requests, a 96.50% completion rate for synthetic cross-stage workflows, and a 96.67% completion rate for workflows based on 60 de-identified patient records. Demonstrated the technical feasibility of LLM-based orchestration by confirming that longitudinal context preservation and schema validation are critical for accurate backend routing and workflow completion. Principles that Guide, Actions that Inform: Agent Evolution via Knowledge Abstraction A study on SAGA, a framework that supports the continuous evolution of LLM agents by progressively abstracting interaction experiences into generalizable principles. Existing external memory-based agents had limitations where empirical knowledge was bound to specific tasks and contexts, restricting their generalization capabilities. The SAGA framework progressively abstracts agent interaction trajectories into principles equipped with episodic descriptions, reusable procedures, and explicit application conditions. It concretizes extracted principles into task-specific instructions and refines actions through feedback, demonstrating improved performance in ScienceWorld and ALFWorld experiments. TEMPEST: Temporal Embeddings for Scalable Driver Identification via Angular Margin Learning A study proposing TEMPEST, a temporal convolutional neural network embedding model based on additive angular margin learning for scalable driver identification. To overcome the limitations of existing triplet-loss methods, angular margin (ArcFace) loss is applied to enforce global class separation in a normalized angular space. Maps 60 seconds of multimodal driving data into 96-dimensional embeddings, supporting dynamic driver enrollment without retraining or classifier recalibration. With a lightweight model of 720K parameters and 2.80 MB size, it achieves 91.71% Rank-1 accuracy on a 45-driver dataset, demonstrating high scalability and cross-session generalization performance. When Does External Guidance Help LLM Reasoning? A Bias-Variance Theory of Guidance-Augmented GRPO This paper presents a bias-variance theoretical framework and optimal weighting rules for reinforcement learning (GRPO) techniques integrated with external guidance to enhance the reasoning capabilities of large language models. Proposed Guidance-Augmented GRPO (GA-GRPO), a unified framework that encompasses existing methodologies combining external guidance (such as expert trajectories and self-explanations). Proved that GA-GRPO achieves an O(1/sqrt(T)) convergence rate, and derived a closed-form optimal weighting rule (lambda-star) that counterbalances bias, along with a minimax lower bound. Experimental results based on Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Base demonstrated comparable or superior performance while reducing computational overhead (GPU hours) by 31% compared to existing methods. Neutrosophic Ensemble Classification for Uncertainty-Aware Bearing Fault Detection: Evidence from Laboratory and Variable-Speed Industrial Benchmarks This study applies neutrosophic uncertainty decomposition techniques to ensemble models to disentangle unwarranted overconfidence from ambiguous predictions in bearing fault diagnosis, evaluating the approach across laboratory and industrial benchmark settings. To overcome the limitations of conventional machine learning confidence scores, an ensemble of Random Forest, XGBoost, and Logistic Regression was decomposed into four neutrosophic metrics (T-hat, F-hat, predictive entropy I1-hat, and decision discordance I2-hat) to quantify uncertainty. On the CWRU benchmark, 100.00% accuracy was achieved across three load conditions following mapping error correction; however, on the JNU benchmark (excluding the 1000 rpm condition), accuracy plunged to 40.64%, with standalone Logistic Regression (57.91%) demonstrating superior generalization over tree-based ensembles. Fusing time-domain and frequency-domain models while measuring Jensen-Shannon divergence outperformed internal entropy (AURC 0.29 vs. 0.36) and accurately captured distribution shifts. Comparative review of hybrid forecasting models for short-term prediction of building thermal load This paper presents a comparative analysis of the performance of various hybrid artificial intelligence models for short-term prediction of building thermal loads. Qualitatively evaluated hybrid techniques reported in existing literature by analyzing their input/output characteristics, key advantages and disadvantages, and parameter identification methods. Comparatively validated the performance of 13 hybrid forecasting models using historical heat demand and weather forecast datasets from Scottish households. Evaluation results showed that the EMD-LSTM-Markov model predicted next-day demand profiles for space heating and domestic hot water with the highest accuracy, though it had limitations in slightly underestimating sudden power spike intervals. Event-Driven ML Pipeline Orchestration for Manufacturing: An AWS Industry Experience Shares three years of industrial operational experience efficiently orchestrating machine learning pipelines in automotive manufacturing processes using event-driven AWS cloud infrastructure. Coordinated the training of physics prediction models and reinforcement learning control policy models by combining Amazon ECS, EC2 GPU capacity providers, SQS, and an admission control Lambda dispatcher for concurrency limiting. Operated over 40,000 real production training jobs, achieving a 72-78% cost reduction compared to always-on GPU infrastructure. Demonstrated through discrete event simulation that admission control is essential to prevent the 65% job loss caused by naive dispatching, and open-sourced the simulator along with Terraform module skeletons. Zero-Shot Visualization: Exploring Text Corpora with User-Prompted Axes This paper proposes the Zero-Shot Visualization (ZSV) methodology and design guidelines for mapping and visualizing document corpora onto axes defined by users in natural language. It introduces the Zero-Shot Visualization (ZSV) task, which enables visual exploration of text corpora by mapping documents onto conceptual axes specified by users in natural language. Evaluating various scoring techniques—including embedding similarity, direct semantic judgment, and conditional likelihood estimation—demonstrated that next-token probability-based scoring offers the best practical balance between semantic fidelity and computational cost. It presents concrete design considerations and guidelines for real-world corpus exploration, such as the necessity of combining binary relevance filtering with step-by-step axes, as well as addressing compound sentiment bias found in off-topic documents. Tree Navigation Without LLM Summaries: A Matched-Cost Study of Hierarchical Retrieval for Long-Document QA This study proposes the NavTree retriever, which performs efficient retrieval for long-document question answering (QA) using only deterministic balanced tree navigation without generating LLM summaries. It reveals that the benefits gained from existing RAPTOR-style summary trees stem from the tree-based navigation structure rather than the generated summary content itself. It proposes NavTree, which incurs zero LLM call costs during indexing and ultimately delivers only leaf chunks to the reader model through hybrid navigation combining lexical and dense retrieval. Under matched-cost evaluation and long-document multi-hop QA settings, NavTree showed the best performance among existing hierarchical retrievers and demonstrated superior performance over BM25 and RAPTOR variants that use summary trees. Component and Dimension Sparsity in Transformer Refusal Mechanisms A study revealing that the refusal mechanism in transformer language models is not distributed across the entire model, but sparsely concentrated in specific components and dimensions. By analyzing refusal steering across four open-weight models, the authors identified a sparse subset of attention and MLP components capable of reproducing the full effect. Refusal directions are concentrated in 28--48% of the top components, confirming that 88--101% of the total steering effect can be preserved. Even within these mechanisms, the effect is concentrated in approximately 50% of the residual stream dimensions, maintaining 85--98% of baseline performance and demonstrating that sparsity operates at both the component and dimension levels. Stabilizing language models under continual learning via condition-anchored distillation This study explores Condition-Anchored Generative Distillation (CAGD), a technique that leverages a small number of previous prompts and distillation from a fixed prior model to prevent performance degradation in language models under continual learning environments. In continual learning scenarios where retaining full past datasets is challenging, the study proposes Condition-Anchored Generative Distillation (CAGD), which matches prediction distributions using a small set of previous prompts and a frozen prior model. Unlike conventional replay methods, it decouples the three roles—selecting behaviors to protect (conditions), exploring relevant states (teacher model generations), and specifying permissible prediction shifts (soft targets)—and applies them to both autoregressive and masked diffusion models. In an evaluation adapting a 219M masked diffusion model across 4 tasks, it significantly reduced the final loss from 2.927 to 1.114, while demonstrating consistent performance preservation on recent facts and natural language instruction following for SMDM and Qwen3 models. Verdicts Without Annotated Evidence: Rejection Sampling or Label-Only Post-Training for Evidence Recovery? This study improves the evidence recovery performance of small language models through post-training and rejection sampling utilizing only final verdict labels without human evidence annotations. Due to the low correlation between verdict agreement rates and actual human evidence span agreement rates, verdict accuracy alone is insufficient to evaluate evidence citation quality. The model post-trained using only verdict labels recorded an accuracy of 0.896 and a span F1 of 0.564, while the rejection sampling method achieved an accuracy of 0.797 and a span F1 of 0.556. Both methods significantly improved the verbatim citation rate from 0.597 before training to 0.729 and 0.701, respectively, even without human evidence annotations. One Model Family, Two Gold-Level Results: Fine-Tuning Nemotron for IOI and IMO Research findings showing that NVIDIA achieved gold medal-level performance at both the International Olympiad in Informatics (IOI) and the International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO) by fine-tuning a single base model, Nemotron 3. By utilizing Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Reinforcement Learning (RL), and feedback-driven inference loops, Nemotron 3 was specialized into expert models for competitive programming and mathematical proof without training a new foundation model. Applying an iterative verification and refinement strategy (GenCorrect) in competitive programming led Nemotron-3-Nano-CC to score 468 points and Ultra-CC to score 535.4 out of 600 based on IOI 2026 standards, surpassing the gold medal threshold. In Olympiad mathematics, through 15,818 problems and 414,890 refined datasets, the model was trained beyond simple answer generation to generate proofs, verify them, and respond to critiques, earning a gold medal level in evaluation by official IMO graders. Show GN: Where is my cross-border package right now? Introducing 'Baboship', an open-source service that helps track the location of international direct purchase parcels safely and free of charge using real logistics data. Unlike conventional simple LLM prediction methods, it precisely tracks parcel locations based on real data from cities, EMS, airports, and shipping logistics. It protects privacy by processing data ephemerally within the browser without requiring the user's residential address. Released as open source, anyone can download or modify the code, reducing the costs associated with paid tracking and prediction services. Strands Decider 2B - Compact Open-Source Decision-Making Model Strands Decider 2B, a 2-billion-parameter open-source decision-making model developed to quickly and efficiently handle agent decision-making tasks such as tool selection and model routing instead of text generation, has been released. Built on Qwen3.5-2B, it replaces the text generation head with an option evaluation pointer head to derive fast decisions and confidence scores in a single forward pass. It is well-suited for hybrid architectures that reduce cost and latency by offloading repetitive agent judgment tasks, such as argument validation prior to tool execution and confirming with users. It ranked at the top tier in 2B-class evaluations on JevBench, and the code, weights, and training data are all released as open source, enabling local hardware deployment. Penguin Mail - Open-source Rust Email Client for Linux Powered by AI Penguin Mail is an open-source Rust email client for Linux that supports multi-account integrated management and local AI assistant capabilities via LM Studio or Ollama. Enables unified management of emails, calendars, and contacts across diverse accounts such as Gmail, Microsoft, and IMAP/POP3 within a single desktop client. Provides an optional AI assistant running locally through LM Studio and Ollama integration, requiring user confirmation before executing major actions. Connects directly to account services without proprietary servers, tracking, or advertisements, and is released as open source under the GPL-3.0-or-later license. Show GN: Moving email phishing simulation drills to phone calls: Unannounced phone calls Prompted by a family member's experience as a victim, a mobile app was developed and released to provide realistic, unannounced voice phishing simulation drills. Enhanced realism by conducting drills via actual phone calls or text messages at random times during weekday daytime hours without prior notice. Responds using LLM classification based on actual reported phishing scripts, offering six phishing scenarios including parcel delivery and institutional impersonation. Released on both major app stores as a free personal app for public interest, primarily targeting middle-aged and older adults. Claude Code's Cleverest Feature Isn't Meant for Users Speculation that Claude Code's next-message suggestion feature could serve as a feedback collection mechanism for model training beyond simple convenience, alongside the developer's clarification. Claude Code's next-message suggestion feature can be interpreted as a mechanism for gathering high-quality RLHF preference data and learning workflow sequences via user edits. Analyses suggest that edit data generated from real-world development workflows is significantly more useful than traditional thumbs up/down feedback or third-party labelers. Anthropic developer edwinarbus clarified that the feature is not designed to collect preference data, but rather to help users maintain their workflow and resume sessions. Looking for developer-friendly inference providers who give you enough API credits to experiment [D] A post by a solo developer who hit API rate limits during multi-agent experiments, asking for recommendations for developer-friendly inference providers that offer sufficient experimentation credits and request limits. The author is building a repository indexing and benchmark generation tool that runs multiple agents in parallel using models such as Llama 3.3 70B and Qwen 2.5. While Together AI was initially sufficient, scaling up the agents quickly led to hitting requests per minute (RPM) and tokens per minute (TPM) limits. Although satisfied with model performance, the author shares the frustration of being unable to conduct meaningful tests due to being a solo developer unable to immediately upgrade to enterprise paid tiers. NeurIPS 26 Event Metadata Deadline [D] Inquiry regarding the deadline for entering event metadata and errors in selecting paper subfields for the NeurIPS conference Inquiring whether the deadline for entering NeurIPS event metadata coincides with the camera-ready submission deadline Raising an issue where all topic settings among metadata fields are displayed as 'None' Sharing an issue where the AI4SocialScience (Economics and Finance) field selected during paper submission disappeared from the list Where to get started if you want to publish papers in Neurips,ACL,ICLR/A* conferences [R] A question regarding the roadmap and preparatory steps required to publish papers at top-tier AI conferences like NeurIPS, ACL, and ICLR, and to gain admission to top-school PhD programs. A 2nd-year AI engineer asks for a step-by-step research approach to start from the basics and publish papers at major A* conferences. The author aims to enroll in a US Computer Science master's program in Fall 2028 and subsequently pursue a PhD at prestigious universities such as Stanford or Princeton. Seeking advice on how to initiate research while working full-time, as well as the key factors needed to be recognized as a promising researcher during PhD admissions. Best Books/Courses/Channels to Leapfrog on AI/ML Material A question asking for recommended learning resources, such as books, courses, and YouTube channels, to quickly catch up on the latest AI/ML developments since 2018–2019. A developer with a gap since 2018–19 inquired about recommended learning resources (books, MOOCs, YouTube channels) to understand the latest AI/ML technology trends. They are looking for roadmap-style resources, similar to TeachYourselfCS, that systematically guide through domain-wide knowledge and historical developments. They asked whether different approaches are needed to understand traditional ML models deployed at scale before 2021–2022 versus the latest LLMs. Mistral says \"Le Chonk\" can challenge the best AI models French AI company Mistral has unveiled Mistral Large 4 (nicknamed 'Le Chonk'), a freely available 1-trillion-parameter model capable of competing with top-performing models from the US and China. Mistral has released a preview version of Mistral Large 4 (Le Chonk), a 1-trillion-parameter model that anyone can use and customize, with an official release scheduled for later this month. While designed to compete with general-purpose models, it is optimized for specialized industrial domains such as coding, cyber defense, manufacturing, finance, and electrical engineering. Its strategy focuses on targeting various niche areas that other research labs do not focus on, thereby maximizing the practical value of the model. Google rolls out improved SynthID AI content detector, now available globally Google has globally launched a dedicated SynthID detection website, improved to detect watermarks from partner companies as well. Google launched a new dedicated website (SynthID.com), allowing anyone to directly check AI-generated watermarks without going through the Gemini interface. Previously, it could only detect Google-specific watermarks, but the improved detector now supports SynthID watermarks from major partners, including images generated by ChatGPT. SynthID is a technology that embeds imperceptible signals into the pixels of AI-generated images and videos as well as audio waveforms, having been applied to over 180 billion images and videos and 240,000 years of audio through Gemini alone. HiPHI: A Large-Scale Benchmark for High-Precision Human Motion and Object Interaction Introduces HiPHI, a large-scale motion capture benchmark dataset covering high-precision human motion and object interactions to bridge the data gap in humanoid robot learning. Presents the necessity for high-precision data that goes beyond the limitations of existing internet videos and motion capture datasets for humanoid robot learning, a core challenge in Embodied AI and Physical AI. Systematically collects extensive whole-body motions using FrameNet, a linguistic framework of human action, and supports learning interactions such as carrying, pushing, and pulling by including synchronized object trajectories and meshes. Demonstrates that reinforcement learning policies trained on this motion capture data scale with data volume and successfully enable simulation-to-real robot transfer (Sim-to-Real transfer). AI in Production: What Breaks, What Works, and Who Approves It? | InfoQ Webinar An announcement for a webinar covering engineering challenges, security risks, and governance issues that arise when deploying and operating AI agents and RAG pipelines in production environments. As AI agents expand into CI environments and internal systems, permission management, output verification, and clarifying accountability in the event of failures are emerging as key challenges. It addresses ways to mediate tensions between platform engineers, who prioritize development speed and autonomy, and security engineers, who seek to define boundaries for data exposure and security. It shares real-world production use cases, including the scope of agent autonomy, pre-production verification frameworks, preventing sensitive data exposure, and operating production RAG systems. Article: Building a Session-Ordered Kafka Pipeline in Go Introduces a case study of building a Kafka pipeline in Go to achieve parallel processing while strictly guaranteeing in-session message ordering in a large-scale conversational AI platform. Because Kafka partition ordering guarantees alone make it difficult to maintain order in a multi-session environment, application-level routing, consistent hashing, and per-session worker architectures were implemented. Retrying failed messages in place and applying a contiguous watermark commit approach prevented message reordering and ensured fault tolerance. To solve blocking issues in a single worker pool, a two-tier worker hierarchy was introduced, achieving independent parallel processing and sequential in-session processing simultaneously. Cloudflare Uses an AI Harness to Probe and Harden Its WAF Covers a case study where Cloudflare deployed state-of-the-art AI models in a controlled testing framework to probe vulnerabilities in its Web Application Firewall (WAF) and harden detection rules. Conducted black-box testing starting with attack payloads previously blocked by the WAF, prompting the AI model to mutate encodings and delivery methods based on feedback from prior responses. Across 45 scenarios, 1,107 mutation attempts yielded 49 valid findings, 48 of which were related to command injection or server-side request forgery (SSRF). Based on these test results, two new detection rules (SSRF - Obfuscated Host, SSRF - Restricted Protocol) were added to Cloudflare's Managed Ruleset, and the existing SSRF - Cloud rule was improved. FBI Warns FortiBleed Remains Active After Amassing 86,644 Fortinet Device Credentials The U.S. FBI and Secret Service have warned that the 'FortiBleed' credential-harvesting campaign, which has compromised credentials for over 86,000 Fortinet devices, remains actively ongoing. A Russian-speaking threat actor exploited reused or leaked account credentials alongside legacy SHA-256 storage methods to secure over 86,644 Fortinet device credentials across 194 countries worldwide. The campaign employs a five-stage attack framework comprising reconnaissance, credential stuffing/spraying, traffic interception via a Go-based sniffing tool (FortigateSniffer), GPU-accelerated cracking, and lateral movement leading to data exfiltration. Compromised credentials are refined through honeypot filtering and organizational valuation assessments, suspected to be the work of an Initial Access Broker (IAB) linked to the INC and Lynx ransomware operations. Anthropic Expands Claude Access for Vetted Cyber Teams as Glasswing Finds 129,000 Flaws Anthropic announced the discovery of large-scale software vulnerabilities through Project Glasswing and unveiled a program expanding access to Claude models with relaxed safety filters for vetted cybersecurity professionals. Anthropic's Project Glasswing discovered over 129,000 verified vulnerabilities between April and July 2026, with more than 33,000 classified as critical or high severity. The Cyber Vetting Program (CVP) has been restructured into three tiers—defensive, red team, and specialized access—granting vetted organizations access to advanced models such as Claude Opus 5.5. According to an analysis by VulnCheck, only about 0.67% (2 out of 300) of the discovered vulnerabilities were exploited in the wild, confirming that actual threat potential must be evaluated selectively even amid a surge in AI-driven vulnerability detection. 100+ Compromised Websites Use Fake Cloudflare Checks to Deliver LunexStealer Over 100 compromised websites are distributing the info-stealing malware LunexStealer by tricking users into executing malicious commands through fake Cloudflare verification pages. The threat group UAC-0277 used the ClickFix technique, displaying a forged Cloudflare security verification screen to prompt visitors to run commands manually. They utilized the EtherHiding technique to retrieve malicious domains and operating modes from smart contracts on the Polygon or Ethereum blockchains. Distributed via malicious MSI packages, LunexStealer drops additional modules—the browser extension LUNARAXE and the auxiliary file system control module NAIVEMESS—to steal credentials, cookies, and files. Google Unveils 'Nano Banana 2.1'... Enhancing Performance and Halving Costs Google has unveiled 'Nano Banana 2.1', an image generation and editing model that enhances high-resolution generation quality and consistency retention while cutting image output costs in half. Developed based on Gemini 3.6 Flash, it enhances visual realism in 1K to 4K resolutions and improves tiling artifacts and editing consistency. Supporting a context window of up to 131,072 tokens and 14 reference images, it enables editing while maintaining the features of up to 4 characters and 10 subjects. Image output costs have been halved to $30 per 1 million tokens, and it is available via the Gemini API, AI Studio, Vertex AI, and more. \"AI Makes Calls on Your Behalf\"… Google Gemini Spotted Preparing Personal Call Proxy Feature Signs have emerged that Google is expanding Gemini's call proxy feature beyond existing business reservations and inquiries to handle simple personal calls with family and friends. An analysis of the latest Google Gemini app update revealed screens related to 'Gemini Calling,' which delivers simple messages or asks about schedules with friends and family. Rather than open-ended conversations, it is likely to function as limited call assistance akin to a 'spoken text message,' such as late notifications or RSVP checks. To prevent unwanted AI calls, an introductory announcement disclosing the AI proxy at the start of the call and detailed user reception and permission controls are being prepared. Google Unveils On-Device 'EmbeddingGemma 2'... Unified Processing of Text, Images, and Audio Google has unveiled 'EmbeddingGemma 2,' a lightweight on-device multimodal embedding model capable of unified processing of text, code, images, video, and audio on devices such as smartphones and PCs. EmbeddingGemma 2 is a 740 million (740M) parameter modular model based on the Gemma 4 architecture, released under the Apache 2.0 license for commercial use. It supports a context length of up to 8,192 tokens and applies Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL) to reduce local vector DB memory usage by up to 6 times. When running the full multimodal model with quantization on the Pixel 11 Pro, it consumes only about 567MB of RAM and supports major tools including MediaPipe, LiteRT, Ollama, and llama.cpp. Stuart Russell's Warning: \"AI Alignment Is Impossible... The Limits of Imitation Learning and RLHF\" AI scholar Professor Stuart Russell warned that current LLM alignment methods based on imitation learning and RLHF have structural limitations and are bound to fail, proposing the 'Assistance Games' model as an alternative. Pre-training that imitates internet data and RLHF methods aiming for short-term rewards induce deception and sycophantic behavior in AI, making complete alignment structurally impossible. Instead of assuming that human objectives are perfectly known, an 'Assistance Games' model that maintains uncertainty about human preferences is necessary. If safety cannot be mathematically and scientifically proven, AI development must be halted, as erroneous pursuit of autonomous objectives could lead to severe socio-economic catastrophes. Anthropic Integrates with Google Workspace... Call 'Claude' Directly Within Documents Anthropic has launched 'Claude for Google Workspace' in public beta, an integration that allows users to call Claude directly within Google Docs, Sheets, and Slides to perform tasks. Through the Google Workspace add-on, users can run Claude directly from a sidebar within documents to edit content, write formulas, create slides, and more. Users can directly control Claude's editing autonomy through options such as 'review changes before applying' and 'allow all edits'. Security controls for Enterprise plans and Workspace-dedicated connectors that can integrate with other business tools like Salesforce are also supported. Google Officially Launches Image Model 'Nano Banana 2.1'... Improved Text Rendering Google has officially launched 'Gemini Nano Banana 2.1', an efficiency-focused image generation model that improves image quality, text rendering, and character consistency while resolving tiling seam artifacts. Google released 'Gemini Nano Banana 2.1', which enhances text rendering, image quality, and character consistency across multi-turn edits while maintaining Flash-tier speed and cost. The model supports up to 14 reference images, fixes tiling seam issues that occurred when generating images with extreme aspect ratios in 2K and 4K resolutions, and supports Search Grounding. Service for the existing 'Gemini 3.1 Flash Image' model will end on October 29, with developers advised to transition to the new model. MongoDB: \"Key to Production AI Lies in Data Connectivity and Agent Operational History Management\" MongoDB emphasized the importance of data modernization, contextual connectivity, and agent operational history management for enterprises to successfully transition AI into production services. To overcome the limitations of enterprise AI adoption, data modernization based on document data models and contextual connectivity across data are essential. Search accuracy in AI applications must be enhanced by combining vector search, keyword search, metadata filtering, and re-ranking. Storing an agent's inputs, tasks, and results in a database to manage history is necessary to ensure traceability and context sharing across multi-agent systems. Building Enterprise AI Through Conversation... Wisenut Accelerates the Popularization of Agents Wisenut has unveiled 'WISE Agent Labs,' a tool that enables the creation and management of enterprise AI agents purely through conversation without coding, aiming to expand into both public and private markets. At 'AI Festa 26,' Wisenut unveiled 'WISE Agent Labs,' which allows users to create and modify enterprise AI agents through conversation without specialized coding. The solution supports Multi-LLM, RAG, MCP integration, and real-time monitoring, and recently received Grade 1 GS Certification. With more than 80 implementation cases across sectors like finance and defense, Wisenut recorded a 71.5% market share in the AI agent category on the Public Procurement Service's Digital Service Mall in the first half of this year. Preventing AI Jailbreaking and Data Leakage: ITCEN Cloit Unveils Enterprise 'Guardrail' ITCEN Cloit has unveiled 'AgentGo Guard,' an enterprise AI security solution that applies two-way security and governance to prompts and responses without replacing existing AI infrastructure. Designed with an architecture that adds security capabilities without replacing existing internal chatbots, RAG, or on-premise LLMs Applies two-way security, including blocking prompt injection and jailbreaks at the input stage, and filtering personal data exposure and harmful content at the output stage Supports diverse deployment environments including public cloud, on-premises, and air-gapped networks, while offering centralized security policy and usage monitoring via a dashboard \"The Next Bottleneck After GPUs is Data\"… Dinoticia Reshapes AI Infrastructure with VDPU Dinoticia presented an AI storage infrastructure powered by a Vector Database Processing Unit (VDPU) to resolve data search and memory bottlenecks in the era of AI agents. As the advancement of AI agents surges data retrieval volume and KV cache, data center bottlenecks are shifting from GPUs to CPU and memory transfer latency. Dinoticia developed the VDPU, a dedicated search semiconductor that boosts search speed by up to 15x compared to conventional CPUs and minimizes latency with zero-copy technology. The company aims to build an AI storage system that rapidly supplies data to GPUs by integrating VDPU hardware with comprehensive memory management software."